[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should preferences storage hold in v1? → Remember username[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more clarifying question before I lay out approaches — this one's about the interface other forms will call, so
  it's the expensive-to-change decision.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the module knows its preferences up front, or is an open bag callers write whatever they like into.

  - [1mDeclared schema[0m — each preference is registered with a key, a default, and a validator.
    [38;5;153mprefs.get('rememberedUsername')[39m always returns a usable value, corrupt or missing data falls back to the default,
    and an unknown key is a bug you catch immediately. Costs: adding a preference means editing the schema, so a new
    form can't just start writing keys.
  - [1mOpen key-value bag[0m — [38;5;153mprefs.set(k, v)[39m / [38;5;153mprefs.get(k)[39m with no registration. Buys total freedom for later forms. Costs:
    no defaults, so every call site handles [38;5;153mundefined[39m itself; no validation, so a bad write from one form silently
    breaks another; key collisions between forms are invisible until they bite.
  - [1mDeclared schema + namespaces[0m — same as the first, but each form registers its own group. Buys collision safety for
    many independent forms. Costs: more machinery than two preferences justify right now.

  My recommendation is [1mdeclared schema[0m. With "other forms will need it later" as the driver, the thing that actually
  protects those forms is defaults and validation at the boundary, not write freedom — the bag pushes that work into
  every consumer and gets it done inconsistently. Registration is also the cheap direction to reverse: a schema can grow
  namespaces later without changing existing call sites, while retrofitting validation onto a bag means auditing every
  writer.
